            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p33-q6-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "In the first scenario, each distinct code is equally likely. In the second, each selection of physical cards is equally likely; repeated C cards give different weightings to letter outcomes.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Distinct codes: there are six distinct letter types, C, O, N, E, P and T. Count codes with all letters distinct, then codes with both C's:\n$$\\begin{align*}N_{\\mathrm{distinct}}&=\\binom{6}{3}3!\\\\N_{\\mathrm{distinct}}&=120.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}N_{\\mathrm{both\\ C}}&=\\binom{5}{1}\\frac{3!}{2!}\\\\N_{\\mathrm{both\\ C}}&=15.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}P_{\\mathrm{code}}(\\mathrm{both\\ C})&=\\frac{15}{120+15}\\\\P_{\\mathrm{code}}(\\mathrm{both\\ C})&=\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSeparate cards: choose both C cards and one of the other five cards, out of all selections of three cards:\n$$\\begin{align*}P_{\\mathrm{cards}}(\\mathrm{both\\ C})&=\\frac{\\binom{2}{2}\\binom{5}{1}}{\\binom{7}{3}}\\\\P_{\\mathrm{cards}}(\\mathrm{both\\ C})&=\\frac{5}{35}\\\\P_{\\mathrm{cards}}(\\mathrm{both\\ C})&=\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe first sample space consists of equally likely distinct codes. The second consists of equally likely selections of physical cards, so identical letters affect the weighting of the letter outcomes.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
